"Textbook: OpenStax <em>University Physics</em>, Vol. {#}, §{#} (polarization) and §{#} (molecular model of a dielectric, Table {#}). Access for free at https://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-{#}.",
"Choose Attracting a neutral insulator under Setups. A neutral ball of radius {#} cm sits on a {#} cm insulating post on the pan of an analytical balance. A charged rod hangs above it; lower it, set its charge, and set the gap from its tip to the top of the ball. Up and down arrows on the picture move the rod by {#} mm (Shift: {#} cm).",
"The balance reads to {#} mg and is tared with the ball on the pan, so a pull upward on the ball makes the reading negative. The rod moves at a steady speed, and a small ring on the display means the balance is still settling. Tare sets the reading to zero with whatever is pulling on the ball at that moment.",
"Record reading stores the ball, the rod’s charge, the gap and the balance reading; Export to worksheet sends the readings here.",
"Blind hides the force readouts and the arrow, leaving the balance. The Mystery insulator keeps its dielectric constant hidden, and choosing it again gives a new one.",
"A uniform, linear dielectric ball with the dielectric constant of Table {#} of the textbook (the metal ball is a perfect conductor), and the rod’s tip as a point charge on the line through the ball’s center.",
"The force is the exact solution for a ball and a point charge, summed over every multipole, not only the induced dipole. The balance converts force to mass with \\(g = 9.81\\) m/s². The rest of the rod, the post and the balance are left out of the electrostatics.",
"The molecules in the picture are drawn huge and their stretching is exaggerated. The marks around the ball show the bound surface charge (free charge on the metal ball).",
"Measure the force with which a charged rod attracts a neutral insulating ball, find how it depends on the rod’s charge, on the distance and on the material, and use it to find the dielectric constant of an unknown insulator.",
"A charged object attracts a neutral one, conductor or insulator. In a conductor, free electrons move to the near side. In an insulator no charge moves through the material, but each molecule is <strong>polarized</strong>: its electron cloud is pulled a little one way and its nuclei the other, or a polar molecule turns to line up with the field. The side of the ball facing a positive rod ends up with a thin layer of negative <strong>bound charge</strong> and the far side with an equal layer of positive bound charge. The ball stays neutral, but its negative side is closer to the rod, where the field is stronger, so the attraction wins. Turn the rod negative and every molecule turns round: the ball is attracted again.",
"How strongly a material polarizes is measured by its <strong>dielectric constant</strong> \\(\\kappa\\) (Table {#}). A ball of radius \\(a\\) in a uniform field \\(E\\) gets a dipole moment",
"\\[p = 4\\pi\\varepsilon_0\\,a^3\\,\\frac{\\kappa - 1}{\\kappa + 2}\\,E,\\]",
"a standard result for a dielectric sphere, which we take as given. For a metal ball the factor \\((\\kappa - 1)/(\\kappa + 2)\\) becomes {#}. The field of the rod’s tip, a charge \\(q\\) at distance \\(D\\) from the ball’s center, is \\(E = kq/D^2\\). A dipole along a field that changes with distance feels a force \\(p\\,dE/dD\\), and \\(dE/dD = -2kq/D^3\\), so the ball is pulled toward the rod with",
"\\[F_{\\text{dip}} = \\frac{2kq^2a^3}{D^5}\\,\\frac{\\kappa - 1}{\\kappa + 2}.\\]",
"Three things stand out: the force does not depend on the sign of \\(q\\) and grows as \\(q^2\\); it falls as \\(1/D^5\\), far faster than Coulomb’s \\(1/D^2\\); and a material with a larger \\(\\kappa\\) is pulled harder, up to the limit of a metal. The formula is the first term of a series. When the ball is close to the rod the field changes noticeably across the ball, higher multipoles add to the pull, and the measured force is larger than \\(F_{\\text{dip}}\\).",
"The balance reads a mass. A pull \\(F\\) upward lowers the reading by \\(F/g\\), so with the reading \\(m\\) in milligrams,",
"\\[F = -m\\,g,\\qquad F\\ (\\mu\\text{N}) = -9.81 \\times m\\ (\\text{mg}).\\]",
"<strong>Apparatus constants.</strong> Ball radius \\(a = 2.00\\) cm, so \\(D = a + s\\) for a gap \\(s\\) between the rod’s tip and the top of the ball. \\(k = 8.988\\times10^9\\) N·m²/C².",
"Predict \\(F_{\\text{dip}}\\) for the polystyrene ball (\\(\\kappa = 2.56\\)) with \\(q = 20\\) nC and a gap of {#} cm. What balance reading does that correspond to?",
